## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Add a persistent passive-dogfooding mode so one `ts: app-server on` preference makes every eligible
Tool Shed command prefer the qualified Codex App Server path without repetitive per-command flags.
When App Server cannot safely continue, record a sanitized diagnostic and continue the same owner
action in the GUI with mutation-aware reconciliation instead of interrupting product work.

### Why It Matters

Sustained Tool Shed development has displaced work on the owner's product projects. Those projects
should become the realistic App Server learning workload: normal work continues, successful and
failed routes produce evidence, and Tool Shed improvements are reviewed later rather than becoming
an immediate detour. Repetitive backend-selection typing is unacceptable human overhead.

### Desired Outcome

The owner can enable App Server preference once, work normally across Tool Shed-enabled projects,
and receive the intended result without stopping for a recoverable backend failure. Tool Shed
retains enough sanitized evidence to distinguish reliable routes from recurring failure patterns,
while preserving strict no-replay and authority boundaries after possible mutation.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Persistent preference and diagnostics are user-local under the resolved Codex home, not committed
  repository or workspace state, and must survive Tool Shed snapshot upgrades.
- Only already-qualified planning, verification, CAMP execution, and executable `next` forwarding
  may prefer App Server; discussion, brainstorming, unsupported roles, and protected actions retain
  their existing GUI or authority boundaries.
- GUI fallback must be automatic for safe pre-mutation and read-only failures. After possible
  mutation it must reconcile the existing journal and Git boundary before continuing, never replay.
- Preference, logging, or fallback failures must not leak prompts, model output, credentials, or
  secrets and must not silently broaden permissions, enable API-key fallback, or authorize release.
- The first milestone will not add a dashboard, database, background service, automatic issue
  filing, automatic Tool Shed repair, or speculative fleet-management system.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| User-local persistent preference | One command survives sessions, upgrades, and workspaces without dirtying Git | Requires cross-platform atomic storage and precedence rules | Chosen direction |
| Repository-local preference | Naturally project-scoped and inspectable | Dirties or commits personal behavior; upgrade and collaboration ambiguity | Rejected for the default |
| Automatic GUI fallback | Keeps product work moving and turns failures into passive learning | Must distinguish pre-mutation, known mutation, and unknown mutation states | Required |
| Sanitized append-only events | Preserves success/failure evidence without interrupting work | Needs bounded fields, privacy rules, and non-blocking writes | Required; start minimal |
| Role-specific circuit breaker | Avoids repeatedly paying for the same known failure | Premature without field data and introduces cooldown/invalidation policy | Defer until evidence justifies it |

## Open Questions

- Should a later release add per-project overrides after user-wide behavior is proven useful?
- What event volume or repeated failure pattern would justify a role-specific circuit breaker?
- Which aggregate report is useful after real event data exists without creating a new maintenance burden?

## Decisions

- Canonical commands will be `ts: app-server on|off|status`; retain `appserver` as a compatibility alias.
- `on` means prefer App Server with immediate safe GUI fallback, not force App Server at all costs.
- `off` restores the current GUI default; a one-command `--gui` override remains available while on.
- Explicit `--app-server` remains the strict diagnostic route unless later evidence supports changing it.
- The preference is user-wide and user-local for the initial implementation.
- M1 will establish persistence, effective routing, mutation-aware fallback, minimal sanitized event
  recording, status, tests, and current documentation. Reporting analytics and circuit-breaking wait
  for observed evidence.

## Don't Forget

- The primary success measure is uninterrupted product-project progress, not App Server utilization
  percentage by itself.
- A diagnostic write failure must never block GUI fallback.
- Successful App Server use is learning evidence too; the event schema should accommodate success
  without requiring a full report command in M1.

